Step 20 — Criticality-Aware SDN Cost Routing

This is where Step 17 + Step 18 + Step 19 come together.

We will not add live data yet. Step 20 uses test patient values. As agreed, before any live-data implementation, I'll ask you first.

The idea is:

Patient/test data
       ↓
Rule-based criticality
       ↓
NORMAL / URGENT / CRITICAL
       ↓
Different QoS weights
       ↓
Delay + Jitter + Loss + Utilization
       ↓
QoS Cost
       ↓
Minimum-cost route

The important difference is that the same network can now produce different routing decisions for different traffic classes.

20.1 What changes with criticality?

Our four network metrics remain:

$$ D=\text{delay},\quad J=\text{jitter},\quad L=\text{loss},\quad U=\text{utilization} $$

But instead of one fixed cost formula for every packet, we use:

$$ C_k=w_{D,k}D_N+w_{J,k}J_N+w_{L,k}L_N+w_{U,k}U $$

where \(k\) is the traffic class.

For this prototype we'll use these experimental networking policies:

Class	Delay	Jitter	Loss	Utilization
NORMAL	0.20	0.10	0.20	0.50
URGENT	0.30	0.20	0.30	0.20
CRITICAL	0.40	0.25	0.30	0.05

All rows sum to 1.0.

The idea is that NORMAL traffic strongly avoids congestion, while CRITICAL traffic places much more emphasis on delay, jitter, and loss.

These are prototype policy weights, not medically validated values.

