20.4 What should happen?

We deliberately created a routing conflict.

Path A — s1 → s2 → s4
Delay       = LOW
Jitter      = LOW
Loss        = LOW
Utilization = HIGH (90%)
Path B — s1 → s3 → s4
Delay       = HIGHER
Jitter      = HIGHER
Loss        = HIGHER
Utilization = LOW (20%)

So there isn't one universally "best" path.

That's important.

NORMAL traffic

NORMAL has:

Delay       weight = 0.20
Jitter      weight = 0.10
Loss        weight = 0.20
Utilization weight = 0.50

Congestion matters heavily.

For Path A, per-link cost is approximately:

$$ 0.2(0.05)+0.1(0.02)+0.2(0.01)+0.5(0.9) =0.464 $$

Two links:

$$ C_A\approx0.928 $$

Path B:

$$ 0.2(0.25)+0.1(0.16)+0.2(0.08)+0.5(0.2) =0.182 $$

Two links:

$$ C_B\approx0.364 $$

So NORMAL should choose:

s1 -> s3 -> s4
CRITICAL traffic

CRITICAL has:

Delay       = 0.40
Jitter      = 0.25
Loss        = 0.30
Utilization = 0.05

Path A per-link:

$$ 0.4(0.05)+0.25(0.02)+0.3(0.01)+0.05(0.9) =0.073 $$

Total:

$$ C_A\approx0.146 $$

Path B per-link:

$$ 0.4(0.25)+0.25(0.16)+0.3(0.08)+0.05(0.2) =0.174 $$

Total:

$$ C_B\approx0.348 $$

So CRITICAL should choose:

s1 -> s2 -> s4

That's exactly the behavior we're trying to demonstrate.

Expected summary

The most important part of the output should look approximately like:

==========================================
STEP 20 ROUTING SUMMARY
==========================================
Patient A  | NORMAL   | s1 -> s3 -> s4
Patient B  | URGENT   | ...
Patient C  | CRITICAL | s1 -> s2 -> s4
==========================================

The URGENT result may depend on the intermediate policy and exact costs; that's fine. What's important for this controlled demonstration is that the policy can produce different cost calculations and path selections by traffic class.

What Step 20 proves

This is much more important than simply saying:

“Critical packets get priority.”

We're actually defining what that means mathematically.

For the same physical SDN topology:

                  s2
                 /  \
                /    \
Patient →      s1    s4 → Destination
                \    /
                 \  /
                  s3

the routing policy can differ:

NORMAL traffic
      ↓
Congestion-sensitive cost
      ↓
s1 → s3 → s4


CRITICAL traffic
      ↓
Latency/reliability-sensitive cost
      ↓
s1 → s2 → s4

That's application-aware SDN traffic engineering.

